course: dictionary
lesson: 5631
title: Limp: walking, floppy and weak
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning, and the words around it show which meaning the writer means. Limp can be a verb, a noun or an adjective. In «The third time, Connor limps off», limps is a verb: he walks unevenly, probably because one leg is hurt.
explain: Look at the clues. The sentence before tells us Connor slipped, so an injured way of walking fits. If the sentence were about a salad leaf, limp would mean soft and floppy instead.
explain: Use the swap check. Put your meaning into the sentence: «The third time, Connor walks unevenly off.» It still makes sense, so limps is right.
fact: Limp can be a verb (to limp), a noun (a limp) or an adjective (limp).
fact: «Limp» as a verb means to walk unevenly because one leg or foot is hurt or stiff.
fact: «A limp» is that uneven way of walking, as in «walk with a limp».
fact: «Limp» as an adjective means soft and floppy, not firm or stiff, like a wet lettuce leaf.
fact: «Limp» can also mean weak, without energy, as in «a limp handshake». Phrases: «go limp», «limp home», «limp along».
exercise: «The third time, Connor limps off.» What does limps mean here? | Jumps high over the wall | Sits down to rest for an hour | *Walks unevenly because one leg is hurt | Runs fast and laughs
exercise: Mum says the salad leaves have gone limp in the fridge. What does limp mean? | Full of energy | *Soft and floppy, not crisp | Bright green and shiny | Frozen solid
exercise: «Her handshake was limp, with no energy.» What does limp suggest? | Strong and firm | Cold and wet | Very loud | *Weak, without energy
test: In «She does not cry or limp, because cats hide pain well», what kind of word is limp? | *A verb, an action of walking unevenly | An adjective describing a cat's fur | A noun, a thing you can hold | A noun for a sore leg
test: What does the phrase «go limp» most likely mean? | Make a loud noise | *Become soft, floppy or without energy | Start running very fast | Stand up straight and tall
exam: Why does the horse limp in the sentence about the stone stuck beside its hoof? | Because the hoof is made of wood | Because it is asleep | Because it is very happy and jumping | *Because a stone causes pain or stiffness in its leg
